  "text_md": "= 2deg gcd f(y), g(y), yl − 1 ,\nFor comparison, consider the GB code described in Ref. [124]. The polynomials for this GB code are:\n, (74)\nÅ\n(75)\n- BB code (Example 3), specified by the following polynomials:\n- g(x,y) = y + y2 + x9,\nplaced on a twisted 6×30 torus with lattice vectors ⃗a1 = (0,30) and ⃗a2 = (6,6). For physical realization, once we have the architecture for the stabilizers of the (3,3)-BB code, we can generate optimal generalized toric codes on various lattices, as listed in Eq. (59).\nand implemented on an untwisted 30 × 6 torus. The stabilizers have a range of 9 in the x-direction (since x25 and x26 can be equivalently treated as x−5 and x−4).\nk = 2dim\nC. Relation to one-dimensional generalized bicycle codes\nSimilarly, the physical construction of the (3,−3)-\n- f(x,y) = x25 + x26 + y3,\n- g(x,y) = 1 + y + y−6.\n- f(x,y) = 1 + x + x−1y−3,\n(72)\n), ⃗a2 = (1,γ), with 0 ≤ γ <\n- f(y) = 1 + y22 + y102,\n- g(y) = 1 + y + y121,\nZ2[y,y−1] ⟨f(y), g(y), yl − 1⟩\nThis code was first proposed in Ref. [18] using the polynomials\n10",
